WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:06.231
Since starting out as Timely Comics in 1939,
Marvel's created countless classics… and

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.400
also done a few things they probably wish
we'd forget.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.300
Here are a few of the biggest missteps that
the comics powerhouse and blockbuster movie

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.360
machine will never be able to erase, try as
they might.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.580
Disney made headlines for all the wrong reasons
when James Gunn was abruptly fired from his

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.900
writer-director gig on Guardians of the Galaxy
Vol. 3.

00:00:25.149 --> 00:00:30.560
Gunn was axed in response to years-old social
media posts full of bad attempts at dark humor.

00:00:30.569 --> 00:00:34.850
For many disappointed fans, it wasn't the
fact that Gunn was fired that was so outrageous

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.200
— show business can be brutal like that.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.600
What bothered most was how quickly Disney
caved to the pressure.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:45.020
As defenders of Gunn have argued, including
the vocal Guardians star Dave Bautista, the

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:50.460
posts only re-emerged as part of a concentrated
campaign by far-right agitators to get Gunn

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.900
fired, particularly alt-right conspiracy theorist
Mike Cernovich.

00:00:54.269 --> 00:00:58.880
Despite efforts by the cast to get the director
reinstated, Disney stood by the decision - and

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.520
some still haven't forgiven them.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.130
For his part, Gunn landed on his feet, being
quickly scooped up by Warner Bros. to write

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:07.620
and potentially direct a Suicide Squad sequel.

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:12.880
But the firing has still left one of the MCU's
best series feeling tarnished, with fans mourning

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:13.880
what could have been.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.300
Marvel made some important strides toward
fair representation in Hollywood with Black

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:23.090
Panther, but sadly it took the studio longer
to find the nerve to do the same for gender

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:23.580
equality.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:28.000
Marvel Studios president Kevin Feige has been
discussing the possibility of a Black Widow

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.009
movie for what seems like forever.

00:01:30.009 --> 00:01:34.220
Speaking at a press conference in support
of the release of Iron Man 2 on DVD back in

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.920
2010, Feige said,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:40.479
"We've already started discussions with Scarlett
about the idea of a solo movie and have begun

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:43.500
putting together concepts, but The Avengers
comes first."

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:48.630
The Black Widow movie was bumped to the bottom
of the pile in favor of new male-led franchises

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.400
and sequels in the years that followed.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:56.640
It wasn't until Warner Bros. and DC proved
with 2017's Wonder Woman that female-led superhero

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.090
movies could work that Marvel got on board
with the idea, greenlighting Black Widow and

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:02.660
fast-tracking Captain Marvel.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.160
But why did it take so long?

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:05.840
Feige told Entertainment Weekly,

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:07.620
"I think there are a lot of reasons.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:11.760
Not the least of which was fighting for many
years the erroneous notion that audiences

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.401
did not want to see a female-led hero [film]
because of a slew of films 15 years ago that

00:02:16.401 --> 00:02:17.140
didn't work.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.590
And my belief was always that they didn't
work not because they were female-led stories,

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:24.730
they didn't work because they were not particularly
good movies."

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:27.600
"Okay, bad, good… gotta be something in
between, right?

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.720
Maybe it's a little more complicated."

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:36.550
The Marvel Cinematic Universe is known for
being a well-oiled machine, making the whole

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:40.880
extended movie universe thing seem easy by
planning ahead years in advance.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.740
That's true for the most part, but the MCU
is far from watertight.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:49.140
Its timeline has some major issues and some
attentive fans have pointed them out.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:53.870
The questions began when viewers noticed that
lines of dialogue and title cards in the Sony-Marvel

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:58.260
collaboration Spider-Man: Homecoming stated
that the movie takes place some eight years

00:02:58.260 --> 00:02:59.860
after the events of The Avengers.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:04.820
This didn't seem to tally, and Avengers co-director
Joe Russo confirmed that it was indeed an

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:05.320
error.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.440
Marvel released an official MCU timeline in
the Marvel Studios: The First 10 Years source

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:10.940
book.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.900
The new, corrected timeline confirmed that
The Avengers took place in 2012 and placed

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.840
Homecoming in 2016.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.800
Unfortunately the new timeline raised more
questions than it answered.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.440
“You weren’t manipulating the space-time
continuum, you were breaking it!”

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:30.580
Dialogue in Infinity War states that the third
Avengers film takes place two years after

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:34.820
Captain America: Civil War, but the timeline
places them just a year apart.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:39.510
ScreenRant writer Thomas Bacon points out
that the most confusing choice is the new

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:40.580
timing of Black Panther.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:46.420
The new official timeline places Civil War
in 2016 and Black Panther in 2017.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:51.700
But the Wakandan king T'Chaka dies in Civil
War and his son T'Challa is named king in

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:53.080
the beginning of Black Panther.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:57.560
So unless the final brawl between Cap and
Iron Man took place around New Year's Eve,

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:02.640
the new timeline is saying that Black Panther
left Wakanda without a king for around a year,

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.460
and that doesn't sound like something he would
do.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.880
Try as it might, Marvel just can't figure
out a way to fix the timeline.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:12.280
"Now is no time at all.”

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:20.170
When Kevin Feige announced that the cast of
Black Panther would be 90 percent African

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:24.520
or African-American, he made it clear that
Marvel movies were going to embrace diversity

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:25.200
going forward.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:30.051
For a while it seemed as though Marvel Comics
was going to follow the same path, but in

00:04:30.051 --> 00:04:35.270
a 2017 interview, Senior Vice President of
Sales David Gabriel appeared to complain about

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:37.180
the company's new emphasis on diversity.

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:42.270
According to Gabriel, sales figures suggested
that diversity wasn't what Marvel fans really

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:42.900
wanted.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.340
The senior VP told ICv2,

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.740
"What we heard was that people didn't want
any more diversity.

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:50.930
They didn't want female characters out there.

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:55.310
[...] We saw the sales of any character that
was diverse, any character that was new, our

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:59.370
female characters, anything that was not a
core Marvel character - people were turning

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:00.420
their nose up against."

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:05.139
Naturally, the interview led to a backlash,
and Gabriel was forced to release a statement

00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:06.560
clarifying his comments.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:11.000
He claimed that Marvel bosses were proud and
excited about the new range of diverse characters,

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:12.520
but hadn't done enough to promote them.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:18.580
In 2017, a Marvel comic made headlines across
the world for all the wrong reasons.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:22.840
The company re-launched its X-Men line in
April of that year, but what should have been

00:05:22.849 --> 00:05:28.259
a time for celebration quickly descended into
controversy thanks to Ardian Syaf, a Muslim

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:32.200
artist who hid political and religious references
in X-Men: Gold #1.

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:37.180
On top of referencing the date of a protest
held in the Indonesian capital of Jakarta,

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:41.940
Syaf drew the X-Men leader Kitty Pryde, a
Jewish superhero, in front of a jewelry store.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:46.660
While the whole sign is legible, fans pointed
out that the first three letters were particularly

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:47.160
prominent.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:53.100
He also drew fan favorite Colossus in a T-shirt
with "QS 5:51" written across the chest, a

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:56.980
reference to an anti-Semitic and anti-Christian
verse from the Koran.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:01.560
It's easy to see why readers were upset about
the message Syaf was secretly peddling, a

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:04.200
message that goes against the inclusive themes
of the X-Men.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:08.000
After predictably firing Syaf, Marvel released
a statement that read,

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.659
"These implied references do not reflect the
views of the writer, editors or anyone else

00:06:12.659 --> 00:06:17.229
at Marvel and are in direct opposition of
the inclusiveness of Marvel Comics and what

00:06:17.229 --> 00:06:19.379
the X-Men have stood for since their creation."

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:23.169
"We're the X-Men, a dated metaphor for racism
in the ‘60s.

00:06:23.169 --> 00:06:24.880
So, respect.

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:26.400
At ease, officer."

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:32.880
Marvel started a very public spat with fans
with 2016's Captain America: Steve Rogers

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:33.889
#1.

00:06:33.889 --> 00:06:37.990
It was revealed at the end of the explosive
issue that Marvel's star-spangled Avenger

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:42.710
turned to the dark side, with Steve Rogers
revealing himself to be a HYDRA agent.

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:47.689
The fact that HYDRA is essentially an offshoot
of the Nazis didn't seem to matter to Marvel,

00:06:47.689 --> 00:06:49.100
though it mattered to fans.

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:55.080
Petitions were started and hashtags like #SayNoToHYDRACap
and even #BoycottMarvel appeared on social

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:55.600
media.

00:06:55.900 --> 00:07:00.100
Marvel kept telling fans to hang tight, and
in the end HYDRA Cap was defeated.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:01.180
But was it worth it?

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:06.500
“Whoever he used to be, the guy he is now,
I don’t think he’s the kind you save.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:08.260
He’s the kind you stop.”

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:13.479
A lot of readers felt like it wasn't worth
it at all, and Polygon gave voice to those

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:15.830
fans, writing during its Secret Empire autopsy,

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:20.539
"It's irresponsible to play sleight of hand
with potentially volatile and offensive imagery

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.720
just because 'it all works out in the end'."

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:27.700
Rock legends Alice Cooper and Kiss had already
starred in comics in the '70s.

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:31.870
The company decided to resurrect the idea
of inking musicians in the mid-'90s, at a

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:34.180
time when comic books were once again big
business.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:37.490
In a detailed piece for Spin, David Grossman
wrote,

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:42.479
"The Marvel Music imprint aimed to not only
revive the forgotten rock-comics genre, but

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:45.480
elevate it to new heights, both artistically
and commercially.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:49.659
[...] In the end, Marvel Music mostly served
as a vehicle for the higher-ups at a newly

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:52.040
rich company to hang out with rock stars."

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:57.160
Comics starring Billy Ray Cyrus, Onyx, the
Rolling Stones, and even Bob Marley were hastily

00:07:57.169 --> 00:08:01.249
put together, but Marvel just couldn't figure
out how to market them, much to the dismay

00:08:01.249 --> 00:08:03.240
of Marvel Music head Mort Todd.

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:08.280
Recruited from Cracked magazine to helm Marvel's
more rock-minded comics, Todd told Spin,

00:08:08.289 --> 00:08:12.319
"They didn't know how to sell anything that
wasn't superheroes [...] So I was infuriated

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:14.300
with Marvel and didn't renew my contract."

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:18.860
Marvel Music's spectacular failure contributed
to one of Marvel's darkest times.

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:24.640
The dud imprint was pulled in 1995 and Marvel
filed for chapter 11 bankruptcy the following

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:25.140
year.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:30.040
Brian Michael Bendis joined the Marvel writing
staff in 2000 and quickly made a name for

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:32.620
himself working on the Ultimate Spider-Man
series.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:37.390
His Marvel career got off to a flying start,
but his relationship with fans turned sour

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:41.940
in 2004 when he was given creative control
over Earth's Mightiest Heroes.

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:46.600
Buoyed by the success of his Spidey run, Bendis
went in hot and decided to shake things up

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:47.220
big time.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:51.610
Instead of easing himself into the title,
he disassembled the Avengers, killing off

00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:55.800
Hawkeye, Ant-Man, and Vision and scattering
the remaining team members.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.160
In 2018, Bendis told CBR,

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:00.500
"Some fans liked it, and some hated it.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:05.040
There were a lot of arguments online, and
a fan said to me, 'We're Avengers fans.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:09.140
All we buy is Avengers, and we have no idea
who you are, but you came over and kicked

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:10.320
all our toys over.'"

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:15.750
In November 2017 - 17 years after he started
working for Marvel - Bendis jumped ship and

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:18.020
signed an exclusive deal with DC Comics.

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:22.540
When he spoke to CBR a few months later, the
new Superman writer pledged that he wouldn't

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:24.870
make the same mistakes that he made on The
Avengers.

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:25.660
He explained,

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:29.560
"It's not that I would have changed the story
of Avengers, but the glee with which I did

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:31.280
things, I could have been more cool with."

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:37.520
Just weeks after Brian Michael Bendis departed
for DC, Marvel's editor-in-chief Axel Alonso

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:39.460
also left the company without explanation.

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:44.500
Veteran writer C.B. Cebulski took his place
at the top, but his reign was destined for

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:45.100
a rocky start.

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:49.890
The week after he was announced as the new
head honcho at Marvel Comics, Cebulski admitted

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:53.490
that he'd previously written under the pseudonym
Akira Yoshida.

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:58.550
Akira Yoshida was once a mysterious character
in the comic book world, gaining notoriety

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:02.810
in the mid-2000s for his work with Marvel,
who were desperate to find foreign writers

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:05.720
that could create authentic stories for the
American audience.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:09.360
As it turns out, Yoshida wasn't a fresh foreign
voice at all.

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:14.500
Cebulski had been masquerading as a Japanese
writer, inventing a full backstory and conducting

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:15.000
interviews.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:19.060
Among the outlets that managed to score a
rare interview with "Yoshida" when he was

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:24.360
active was CBR, and the website's Assistant
Editor Jon Arvedon was not happy when he found

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:25.960
out about Cebulski's deception.

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:27.020
Arvedon wrote,

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:31.680
"One or more people were fully dedicated to
perpetuating this nearly endless trail of

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:36.880
dishonesty to maintain the false aura of authenticity
surrounding the mysterious Akira Yoshida.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:41.611
Cebulski finally owning up to it is certainly
a small step in the right direction, but the

00:10:41.620 --> 00:10:46.490
damaged reputations and the harm done to writers,
artists and readers may take years to repair."

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:49.220
"Caught in my own web of lies."

